    "description": "Social participation concerns how people contribute to collective life through organizations, voluntary service, and other shared activities. Humanitarian volunteering provides a documented example in Iran, although organizational accounts cannot establish participation rates across society.",
    "tags": ["Social participation", "Volunteering", "Humanitarian response", "Community life"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T04:25:14Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T04:25:14Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Social Participation in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore documented humanitarian volunteering in Iran and the evidence needed to understand wider patterns of social participation."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "participation-and-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Participation through organized service",
        "content": "Humanitarian volunteering offers a concrete entry point into social participation in Iran. The International Committee of the Red Cross, or ICRC, distinguishes its own operations from those of national Red Cross and Red Crescent societies: its Iran volunteering page states that it does not offer volunteer positions and directs prospective volunteers to the Iranian Red Crescent Society. The distinction matters because these organizations belong to the same humanitarian movement but do not provide identical routes into participation. [s1](https://ir.icrc.org/en/icrc-in-iran/volunteership/)\n\nAn organization can document how volunteers contribute to its work without providing a representative picture of the population. Evidence about humanitarian service should therefore be read as evidence of a particular form of collective activity. It cannot, by itself, establish how frequently Iranians participate in neighborhood groups, professional associations, cultural organizations, or other settings.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "humanitarian-example",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A documented example from emergency response",
        "content": "An account issued by the International Federation of Red Cross and Red Crescent Societies, or IFRC, describes Iranian Red Crescent activities during hostilities, with an explicit reporting cutoff of 2 April 2026. It reports volunteers working alongside emergency teams and medical personnel. In Salmas, a city in West Azerbaijan province, rescuers reportedly worked with the fire department to extract a person trapped beneath rubble and transfer the survivor for treatment. [s2](https://www.ifrc.org/article/through-rubble-and-loss-iranian-red-crescent-teams-continue-saving-lives-amid-escalating)\n\nThe same account describes psychosocial support for displaced families in West Azerbaijan and a barber volunteering haircuts for rescuers. These examples illustrate different contributions: specialist rescue, emotional support, and an occupational skill offered to sustain responders. They also show why participation cannot be understood solely through formal membership. [s2]\n\nThese are accounts from an organization supporting the response, rather than an independent evaluation. They establish what IFRC reported at that date; they do not measure national volunteering prevalence or demonstrate that assistance reached every community equally. Emergency mobilization also should not be treated as a measure of ordinary participation between crises. A fuller account requires evidence on routine associations, informal mutual assistance, access barriers, and differences between communities.",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      }
    ],
